      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of the OIML certificate carefully.\n\nKey information:\n- Certificate number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01, revision 7\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 3528374\n- Page total: 12\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 30 August 2022 (2022-08-30)\n- OIML issuer: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Applicant and Manufacturer: ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n- Certified type: electromagnetic water meter, Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\n- Recommendation: R 49-1 (2013) → canonical id \"R49-1\"? Hmm. The rule says id canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For R49, it's split into parts: R 49-1. So canonical could be \"R49\" with... Actually the recommendation is \"R 49-1 (2013)\". I'll use id \"R49-1\", edition 2013. Or maybe id \"R49\" and edition 2013, part... The schema has id (canonical), edition (int), amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes. I'll use \"R49-1\" as the id since it's a part of R49. Hmm, canonical examples: \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For R49-1, I think \"R49-1\" is fine. Edition: 2013.\n- Accuracy class: 1 and 2 → [1, 2]\n\nTest reports:\n- No. NMi-2254799-01 dated 14 August 2020, 235 pages\n- No. NMi-2254799-03 dated 30 September 2020, 44 pages\n- No. NMi-2254799-04 dated 24 December 2020, 180 pages\n- No. NMi-2564429-01 dated 02 August 2021, 26 pages\n- No. NMi-2496280-01 dated 26 August 2021, 29 pages\n- No. NMi-2496280-02 dated 23 November 2021, 25 pages\n\nRole: these are \"tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\" — these are the OIML Type Evaluation Report(s). I'd classify them as \"type_evaluation_report\".\n\nDocumentation folder no. T11851-6 — could be a documentation_file in test_reports.\n\nCharacteristics (Table 1, type level):\n- measuring_principle: Electromagnetic\n- accuracy_class: 1 and 2 (maybe just string \"1 and 2\" or omit since it's in recommendation)\n- environmental_class: \"O\" (installed outdoors) → value \"O\" with note? Actually \"O (installed outdoors)\" — value \"O\". Hmm, environmental class O for water meters per R49... I'll keep value \"O\" and maybe a note. The schema doesn't have notes per value except footnote_markers. I could put value \"O (installed outdoors)\" as string. I'll keep it simple: value \"O\". Actually to not lose info, value could be \"O (installed outdoors)\". Let me use value \"O\" — hmm. The instruction: don't fabricate, extract what's there. I'll use \"O (installed outdoors)\" as string value? That mixes. I think value \"O\" is the class; the parenthetical is descriptive. I'll include as string \"O (installed outdoors)\". Hmm... Let me think about the cleanest: measuring_instrument characteristic environmental_class: {value: \"O\"}. But then \"installed outdoors\" lost. Alternatively add a separate characteristic environmental_class_description. I'll just use the string \"O (installed outdoors)\" — acceptable for a string value.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — cleaner: environmental_class: \"O\". I'll keep descriptive in description? There's no description field for characteristics. I'll go with \"O (installed outdoors)\".\n\nHmm, but strictness says separate value from unit. Environmental class is a string anyway. OK.\n\n- electromagnetic_environment: E2\n- mechanical_class: M1\n- temperature_range_ambient: -25 °C / +55 °C → {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C. Note \"(for both flow sensor and flow transmitter)\" — string value can't hold note; I could add note in... I'll just record range and maybe keep note as part of a description? I'll drop the note or make value string? Rules say ranges become {min,max}. I'll do {min:-25, max:55}, unit_symbol \"°C\". The note can be omitted or included as separate characteristic. I'll omit; it's minor. Actually maybe I can add a footnote-like note? No, footnotes are actual footnotes. I'll skip.\n\n- water_temperature_class: T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C) → value \"T50\"? The class is T50 with range 0.1–50 °C. I'd record water_temperature_class: \"T50\" and maybe water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C. Note: \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\" → min 0.1 max 50. Convert comma decimal. I'll add both.\n\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa (16 bar) → map value 1.6, unit MPa; separate characteristic max_admissible_pressure_bar? Or map with additional parenthetical. I'll do maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} and maximum_admissible_pressure_bar: {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}. Or include just the primary. The rule about 150% of Emax suggested separate characteristic for reference. I'll do both.\n\n- orientation: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\" → string value \"All positions\". Keep as string.\n\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream), except for full bore sizes DN250 and larger which have U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream...)\" — this is config-dependent! This fits config_level with axis \"bore_size\" or \"meter_size\": condition \"DN250 and larger (full bore)\" vs \"other sizes\". Let me use config_level:\n  attribute: flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis: \"bore_size (full bore)\"\n  values: [{condition: \"sizes below DN250\", value: {value: \"U0 and D0\", unit_symbol: null}}, {condition: \"full bore DN250 and larger\", value: {value: \"U3 and D0\"}}]\n  \n  Actually config_level values shape: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. The example in rule 5: use `characteristics.config_level` with `axis`. I'll do:\n  ```json\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\",\n    \"axis\": \"bore type / size\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore sizes smaller than DN250\", \"value\": {\"value\": \"U0 upstream / D0 downstream\"}},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore sizes DN250 and larger\", \"value\": {\"value\": \"U3 upstream / D0 downstream\"}}\n    ]\n  }\n  ```\n  Hmm wait — reduced bore sizes? The text says \"except for full bore sizes DN250 and larger which have U3 and D0\". So reduced bore presumably U0/D0 everywhere? Ambiguous. The condition given: default U0/D0, except full bore DN250+ → U3/D0. I'll phrase conditions accordingly: condition \"all sizes except full bore DN250 and larger\" → U0/D0; condition \"full bore DN250 and larger\" → U3/D0.\n\n  Also note \"0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream\" — straight lengths. Could add as characteristics but it's part of the class definition. Keep simple.\n\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → value string true? Or \"intended to measure reverse flow\". I'll use value: true? Hmm, that's interpretation. Use string \"intended to measure reverse flow\". Or boolean true with attribute reverse_flow_capability. I'll do value: true. Hmm, rules say numbers or strings; boolean not listed but fine. I'll use string \"yes\"? Let me just do {\"value\": true}. It's clean. Actually to be safe, string \"intended for reverse flow measurement\"? I'll go with true.\n\n- pressure_loss_class: varies by bore type and size — definitely config_level:\n  axis: \"bore type and meter size\"\n  values:\n  - reduced bore DN40/DN50 → Δp40 (0.40 bar)\n  - reduced bore >DN50 → Δp63 (0.63 bar)\n  - (virtual) full bore DN40/DN50 → Δp10 (0.10 bar)\n  - (virtual) full bore DN65–DN200 → Δp16 (0.16 bar)\n  - (virtual) full bore DN250+ → Δp10 (0.10 bar)\n  \n  Values: pressure loss class like \"Δp 40\" plus numeric 0.40 bar. I'll record value as string \"Δp 40\" and add pressure value? Maybe value: {\"value\": \"Δp 40\", \"unit_symbol\": null} with extra field? The structured value shape is fixed {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. I could record the bar value too as separate config_level attribute \"pressure_loss\" with unit bar. That gives:\n  - attribute pressure_loss_class: values with \"Δp 40\" etc.\n  - attribute pressure_loss: values with 0.40 bar etc.\n  \n  That doubles entries but is thorough. I'll do both? Might be verbose but fine. Alternatively just class with the bar in the value string \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\". Simpler: keep one config_level attribute \"pressure_loss_class\" with value string \"Δp 40\" and another \"pressure_loss\" numeric. Let me include both attributes.\n\n  Note: Δp symbol — avoid LaTeX. \"Δp\" is fine Unicode. Canonical label: pressure_loss_class.\n\n- power_supply: three variants:\n  - AC mains: 85–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz → split: power_supply_voltage_ac: {min:85,max:240} V; power_supply_frequency: {min:50,max:60} Hz; power_supply_type: \"AC\"\n  - Renewable energy power (DC powered): 6–32 V → dc supply voltage range 6–32 V\n  - Replaceable battery: 2x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V) → battery: 2 cells × 3.6 V; operating range 2.9–3.6 V.\n\n  These are power supply variants (config axis: power supply type). Could model as config_level attribute \"power_supply_voltage\" with axis \"power supply\":\n  - condition \"AC mains\": {min:85,max:240} V\n  - condition \"DC (renewable energy)\": {min:6,max:32} V\n  - condition \"replaceable battery (per cell)\": 3.6 V\n  \n  And frequency config: AC only 50/60 Hz.\n  \n  I'll do config_level entries:\n  - attribute: power_supply_voltage, axis: power_supply_type, values: [AC mains 85–240 V, DC renewable 6–32 V, battery 3.6 V (2 cells)]... battery per cell 3.6. Hmm \"2x 3.6 V\" → 2 cells of 3.6 V. I can add attribute battery_cell_count: 2 and battery_voltage: 3.6 V, battery_operating_range 2.9–3.6 V.\n  \n  Let me structure:\n  config_level:\n  1. {attribute: \"power_supply_voltage\", axis: \"power supply type\", values: [\n     {condition: \"AC mains\", value: {min:85,max:240}, unit_symbol:\"V\"},\n     {condition: \"DC (renewable energy powered)\", value: {min:6,max:32}, unit_symbol:\"V\"},\n     {condition: \"replaceable battery\", value: 3.6, unit_symbol:\"V\"}  // per cell, 2 cells\n  ]}\n  2. {attribute: \"power_supply_frequency\", axis: \"power supply type\", values: [{condition: \"AC mains\", value: {min:50,max:60}, unit_symbol:\"Hz\"}]}\n  \n  type_level additions:\n  - battery_cell_count: 2\n  - battery_operating_voltage: {min:2.9, max:3.6} V\n  \n  Good.\n\n- software identification: software part codes with versions and checksums. This is a lot. Options: put in components? No — it's firmware. Could record as type_level characteristics: software_part_code with versions... Multiple part codes each with multiple versions. I could represent as matrix or just type_level strings. Simplest: type_level characteristic \"software_identification\" as a string listing? Rules discourage giant strings but it's textual data. Alternatively components[] with role \"software\"? Not really a component.\n\n  I'll create components entries? Hmm. Better: type_level characteristics:\n  - software_part_code_1: \"3KXF208402U0113\" with versions... can't nest.\n  \n  Maybe matrix_tables: name \"Approved software versions\", columns [software_part_code, version, checksum], rows... That fits well! matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables. Rows:\n  - 3KXF208402U0113, 03.00.03, B26AFE6B\n  - 3KXF208402U0113, 03.00.04, 6E98963A\n  - 3KXF208402U0113, 03.02.00, E12E5C04\n  - 3KXF208402U0113, 03.03.02, D4D1E40B\n  - 3KXF208402U0113, 03.03.03, CEEF45F1\n  - 3KXF208402U0313, 02.00.02, 84EBD1C8\n  - 3KXF208402U0513, 02.00.01, C28EAA16\n  \n  I'll do that. Version as string \"03.00.03\". Checksum string.\n\n- checking_facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\" → type_level string.\n\nModel family: The certified type is AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4* with X ∈ {1,3}, Y ∈ {1,2,8,9}, Z ∈ {R,V,F}. That's a combinatorial family: X (standard/advanced), Y (integral/remote, optionally sensor only), Z (reduced bore/virtual full bore/full bore). Models enumerated? The Type designations: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\" and \"FET4*\". The family covers combos. model_family: family_name \"AquaMaster4\", models[] — should I enumerate 2×4×3=24 variants of FEW4XY.Z plus FET4 variants? That may be overreach. But rule 4 says detect family. The attribute axes X, Y, Z are described. I could model as config axes:\n- X: 1 (standard) or 3 (advanced)\n- Y: 1, 2, 8, 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\n- Z: R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore), F (full bore)\n\nThese are designation-code axes. I'll include model_family with family_name \"AquaMaster4\" and models being the designation axes? The models[] entries have model_id, label, attributes{}. Enumerating 24 variants seems excessive but arguably complete. The table family (Table 2/3) enumerates meter sizes DN40–DN600 — that's the size dimension (Q3 values per size and class). Tables 2 and 3 (Table 3 header appears but content not in this fragment — \"Reduced bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\" heading only, table content presumably on next page, not included). So in THIS fragment only Table 2 content (reduced bore, AC mains powered) is present.\n\nThe models in Table 2: DN40 (class 1, class 2), DN50 (1,2), DN65 (1&2), DN80, DN100 (1,2), DN125 (1,2), DN150, DN200, DN250 (1,2), DN300 (1,2). Page 4 continues: DN350 (1,2), DN400, DN450, DN500 (1,2), DN600 (1,2). Both page-4 table is a continuation of Table 2 (same headers). So the full Table 2 across pages 3–4 is present in the fragment. Include all rows.\n\nSo model matrix: models keyed by meter size and accuracy class. Flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 and ratio Q3/Q1 vary per (size, class). This is a 2-D matrix: size × class.\n\nHow to model? model_level entries with model = \"DN40 class 1\" etc. Or matrix_tables with rows {meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio}. I'll do matrix_tables (matches R137 example) AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level.\n\nmodel_family.models: one per size+class combo? Or per size? The R137 example made one model per size. Here class also varies flow rates. I think model_id per size-class combination: \"DN40/1\", \"DN40/2\"... Alternatively models per DN size, and use config_level for accuracy class axis. Hmm.\n\nGiven ratio Q3/Q1 differs per class (500 vs 1000), the model variant is size+class. I'll create models like {\"model_id\": \"DN40 class 1\", \"label\": \"Reduced bore, AC mains powered, DN40, accuracy class 1\", \"attributes\": {\"q3_q1_ratio\": 500}} — attributes optional.\n\nActually, simpler: models list with model_id \"DN40-1\" ... and let model_level characteristics carry values. And matrix_tables preserving the full table.\n\nNumber of models: sizes and class combos from Table 2 (pages 3–4):\nDN40: 1, 2\nDN50: 1, 2\nDN65: 1&2 (same values for both classes; ratio 1000)\nDN80: 1&2\nDN100: 1, 2\nDN125: 1, 2\nDN150: 1&2\nDN200: 1&2\nDN250: 1, 2\nDN300: 1, 2\nDN350: 1, 2\nDN400: 1&2\nDN450: 1&2\nDN500: 1, 2\nDN600: 1, 2\n\nFor \"1 & 2\" rows, both classes share the same values. For model matrix, I can treat each row as a model entry where model is \"DN65 (class 1 & 2)\" or expand to two models with identical values. Hmm. The example R137 kept one model per size. For combined rows, maybe treat model_id as the DN size with attributes including class \"1 & 2\". For split rows (DN40 class1 vs class2), the model_id must include class.\n\nConsistent approach: model_id = \"<DN> class <n>\" for every row: DN65 class 1 and DN65 class 2 as separate models with same flow values, ratio 1000 each. That's cleaner for the matrix. Total models: DN40:2, DN50:2, DN65:2, DN80:2, DN100:2, DN125:2, DN150:2, DN200:2, DN250:2, DN300:2, DN350:2, DN400:2, DN450:2, DN500:2, DN600:2 = 30 models. That's a lot but consistent and machine-usable.\n\nAlternatively keep matrix_tables rows exactly as in the table (15 rows, some with \"1 & 2\") and model_level with values per model where model ids combine. The rules emphasize model_level for varying attributes. I'll do 30 models? Hmm, that's heavy. Let me reconsider: maybe model per size, with accuracy-class-dependent attributes handled via config_level axis \"accuracy_class\". The characteristics.config_level: attribute q3_q1_ratio, axis accuracy_class... but values also depend on size. Multi-axis: config_level values {condition, value} where condition could be \"class 1\" applied across all sizes with formula? For DN40: class1 ratio 500, class2 ratio 1000; DN65: both 1000. So ratio per size+class: DN40:500/1000, DN50:500/1000, DN65:1000, DN80:1000, DN100:500/1000, DN125:500/1000, DN150:1000, DN200:1000, DN250:500/1000, DN300:500/1000, DN350:500/1000, DN400:500, DN450:500, DN500:500/1000, DN600:500/1000.\n\nQ1/Q2 differ by class where ratio differs (Q2/Q1=1.6, Q3 fixed per size). So really model = size + class.\n\nI'll go with 30 models with ids like \"DN40-class1\". And model_level characteristics:\n- q1 (m³/h), q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio\n- Also meter_size_dn: 40..600 (numeric) and accuracy_class per model.\n\nThat's 30 values per attribute × 5 attributes = 150 entries. Long JSON but correct. Alternatively use matrix_tables rows and lighter model_level. The schema says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables AND populate model_family/models and model_level. The R137 example populated model_level with all three attributes for 2 models. With 30 models, it's long but fine.\n\nHmm, wait — should I include accuracy_class as a model attribute and/or model_level? accuracy class is already in recommendation.accuracy_classes [1,2] (whole cert). Per-model class: include in model attributes: {\"accuracy_class\": 1}.\n\nAlso each model is under the configuration \"Reduced bore – AC mains powered\" (Table 2 heading). Note Table 3 (battery/renewable) heading present but no data rows in this fragment. The note after page-4 table says flow rates can be freely chosen within limits — that's a general note; could go to matrix_tables description or footnote-ish. I'll put in matrix_tables description.\n\nLet me now define matrix_tables entry:\nname: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments — reduced bore, AC mains powered\"\ncolumns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]\nrows (as in table, \"1 & 2\" rows kept with accuracy_class \"1 & 2\"? or expand). For matrix_tables fidelity, keep rows as in the table: accuracy_class: \"1 & 2\" for combined rows. But then models differ... I could keep rows exactly as the source (15 rows) — matrix_tables is the \"preserve\" structure, fidelity matters. And model_level/models get expanded per size+class.\n\nRows:\nDN40 1: 0.05, 0.08, 25, 31.25, 500\nDN40 2: 0.025, 0.04, 25, 31.25, 1000\nDN50 1: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\nDN50 2: 0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000\nDN65 1&2: 0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000\nDN80 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000\nDN100 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\nDN100 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\nDN125 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\nDN125 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\nDN150 1&2: 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000\nDN200 1&2: 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000\nDN250 1: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\nDN250 2: 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000\nDN300 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\nDN300 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\nDN350 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\nDN350 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\nDN400 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\nDN450 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\nDN500 1: 8, 12.8, 4000, 5000, 500\nDN500 2: 4, 6.4, 4000, 5000, 1000\nDN600 1: 12.6, 20.16, 6300, 7875, 500\nDN600 2: 6.3, 10.08, 6300, 7875, 1000\n\nDecimal commas converted: 0,05→0.05; 0,08→0.08; 0,025→0.025; 0,04→0.04; 31,25→31.25; 0,128→0.128; 0,064→0.064; 0,063→0.063; 0,1008→0.1008; 78,75→78.75; 0,1→0.1; 0,16→0.16; 0,32→0.32; 0,512→0.512; 0,256→0.256; 0,4→0.4; 0,64→0.64; 0,63→0.63; 1,008→1.008; 787,5→787.5; 3,2→3.2; 1,6→1.6; 2,56→2.56; 5,12→5.12; 12,8→12.8; 12,6→12.6; 20,16→20.16; 10,08→10.08; 6,3→6.3; 6,4→6.4.\n\nDN400 row: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500 — wait check ratio: Q3=2500, Q1=5 → ratio 500. Yes.\n\nDN350 class 2: Q1 1.6, Q2 2.56, Q3 1600, Q4 2000, ratio 1000. Yes (same as DN300 class 2).\n\nNow models list: 30 entries. Model ids: \"DN40 class 1\", \"DN40 class 2\", \"DN50 class 1\", ..., \"DN600 class 2\". For combined rows, create both class 1 and class 2 models with same flow values.\n\nmodel_level entries: attributes q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio with 30 values each. Also maybe meter_size (DN numeric) and accuracy_class. I'll include meter_size_dn and accuracy_class as model_level too? The example put only varying attributes. accuracy_class and size vary across models by definition. Including them makes each model self-describing. I'll include: q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio. And in models[].attributes: {\"accuracy_class\": 1, \"nominal_size_dn\": 40}. That covers it without duplicating in model_level.\n\nHmm, models[].attributes — schema shows attributes{} in models. Good, put nominal_size_dn and accuracy_class there. And powering/power config: Table 2 is \"Reduced bore - AC mains powered\" — that's a config of the table, not of model. I'll note in matrix description and maybe in model attributes: {\"bore\": \"reduced\", \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\"}. Include for completeness.\n\nWait — but also there's the type designation family FEW4XY.Z with Z ∈ {R, V, F}. Reduced bore = Z=R. Virtual full bore = V, full bore = F. Table 2 only covers reduced bore. Table 3 (battery) not in fragment. So the models from Table 2 correspond to Z=R, AC mains powered variants. I'll reflect that in attributes.\n\nType designations: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\" and \"FET4*\" — the \"*\" footnote explains wildcard X, Y, Z. This footnote should be recorded in footnotes[]: marker \"*\", text: \"With X being 1 or 3 (standard or advanced), Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only) and Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" (it says \"water meter\"; schema example categories include \"Gas meter\" etc., so \"Water meter\")\n- type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\"] — hmm, maybe [\"FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4\"]? The type is \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". I'll use [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\"] or literally [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\"]. I'll go with [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\"] — but asterisk marker might be confused; footnotes marker \"*\". Fine.\n- module_designation: null — not mentioned. Omit.\n- description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\" — description free-text: \"Electromagnetic water meter\" — also measuring_principle char. I'll include description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\".\n\nCertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"3528374\"\n- page_total: 12\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2022-08-30\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\" (from \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\" and number ...NL1...)\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- person_title: null (Certification Board? The signature block says \"Certification Board\" — that's the board, not title. I'll omit person_title or set \"Certification Board\"? Hmm, \"Certification Board\" appears above signature area. Could be the title of the signer. I'll set person_title: \"Certification Board\"? Risky. I'll omit.)\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\napplicants: [ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom]\nmanufacturers: same (Applicant and Manufacturer).\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49-1\" — hmm canonical example \"R60\". For part recommendation, \"R49-1\" seems right. Edition: 2013. amendment: null. scheme: \"A\". accuracy_classes: [1, 2] — as numbers or strings? \"1 and 2\". I'll use [1, 2] numbers? Accuracy classes 1 and 2 (water meters per R49 are class 1/2). Use numbers? Could be strings. I'll use [1, 2] as integers... Actually schema just says accuracy_classes[]. Integers fine.\n\ntest_reports: role — these are the Type Evaluation Report results. The certificate says \"conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". These are the OIML Type Evaluation Report(s). role: \"type_evaluation_report\". ids:\n- NMi-2254799-01, 2020-08-14, 235 pages\n- NMi-2254799-03, 2020-09-30, 44 pages\n- NMi-2254799-04, 2020-12-24, 180 pages\n- NMi-2564429-01, 2021-08-02, 26 pages\n- NMi-2496280-01, 2021-08-26, 29 pages\n- NMi-2496280-02, 2021-11-23, 25 pages\n\nAlso \"Documentation folder no. T11851-6\" → test_reports entry with id \"T11851-6\", role \"documentation_file\". No date/pages. Include.\n\nrevision_history: The number says \"revision 7\" — but no revision history table in fragment. Should I add revision_history entry revision \"7\"? There's no date/changes for it. Rule 8: don't fabricate. The revision number is part of cert number. I'll omit revision_history since no history data. Hmm, but could add {revision: \"7\"}? Without date/changes it's incomplete; I'll omit — certificate.number already contains \"revision 7\"? Actually number field \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — I could keep number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and maybe add \"revision\": not in schema. Schema certificate has no revision field. revision_history has revision/date/changes. I'll omit revision_history entirely (no data). Or include revision \"7\" with null date/changes? Rule 8 says omit if not present. The revision number IS present but its history isn't. I'll skip.\n\nFootnotes: the \"*\" explanation. marker \"*\", text as above.\n\nNow type_level characteristics list:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\" (string)\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\"? Already in recommendation. Include as {value: \"1 and 2\"}? I'll include for completeness as string \"1 and 2\". Hmm, or skip. Table 1 lists it. I'll include.\n- environmental_class: \"O (installed outdoors)\" — I'll split: value \"O\" plus note? Just string \"O (installed outdoors)\". Hmm, let me do value \"O\" and add separate type_level \"environmental_class_description\": \"installed outdoors\"? That seems overkill. I'll do value: \"O (installed outdoors)\". Acceptable as string value.\n\nActually wait — R49 environmental classes are O, X, P...? Class O is outdoor. String fine.\n\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55}, \"°C\". Note \"(for both flow sensor and flow transmitter)\" — I'll include as part of description? Drop. Hmm, maybe include a footnote-like note. I'll just include the range; mention in a type_level string? Skip the parenthetical.\n\nActually I could keep it lossless: add separate characteristic? No — I'll skip the parenthetical to keep clean. Hmm, \"Don't fabricate\" — dropping info is allowed (only fabricating forbidden). Fine.\n\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, \"°C\" (from \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\")\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 \"MPa\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_bar: 16 \"bar\" — label maybe \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alternate\"? I'll use \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\".\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"\n- reverse_flow: true\n- checking_facility_type: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class config entries (below)\n- power supply config entries (below)\n- battery_cell_count: 2\n- battery_voltage_nominal: 3.6 \"V\" (per cell)\n- battery_operating_voltage_range: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6} \"V\"\n\nconfig_level:\n1. pressure_loss_class, axis \"bore type and meter size\":\n   - {condition: \"reduced bore, DN40–DN50\", value: {\"value\": \"Δp 40\"}},\n   - {condition: \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\", value: {\"value\": \"Δp 63\"}},\n   - {condition: \"virtual or full bore, DN40–DN50\", value: {\"value\": \"Δp 10\"}},\n   - {condition: \"virtual or full bore, DN65–DN200\", value: {\"value\": \"Δp 16\"}},\n   - {condition: \"virtual or full bore, DN250 and larger\", value: {\"value\": \"Δp 10\"}}\n2. pressure_loss, axis same:\n   - 0.40 bar, 0.63 bar, 0.10 bar, 0.16 bar, 0.10 bar respectively.\n3. flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis \"bore type and size\":\n   - {condition: \"all sizes except full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream / 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n   - {condition: \"full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream / 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n   Simplify strings: \"U0 upstream / D0 downstream\"? The original: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\". I'll use value \"U0 / D0\" hmm. Let me use strings: \"U0 and D0\" and \"U3 and D0\". The straight-run requirement \"0 x DN / 3 x DN\" is embedded. I could add config attribute \"straight_length_upstream\": \"0 x DN\" etc. I'll include the full strings to be lossless: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\". OK.\n4. power_supply_voltage, axis \"power supply type\":\n   - {condition: \"AC mains\", value: {min:85, max:240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n   - {condition: \"DC (renewable energy)\", value: {min:6, max:32}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n   - {condition: \"replaceable battery\", value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n   Wait battery: \"2x 3,6 V (operating range: 2,9 - 3,6 V)\". The 3.6 V is per cell nominal, 2 cells. If I put 3.6 V for battery condition, add note. Alternatively battery condition value: 7.2? No — don't compute. I'll put condition \"replaceable battery (2 cells)\", value 3.6 V. And battery_operating_range type_level.\n5. power_supply_frequency, axis \"power supply type\": {condition: \"AC mains\", value: {min:50, max:60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n\nNote the config_level value shape: the rule says values follow {condition, value, unit_symbol}. In rule 5 example: `{condition, value, unit_symbol}` — but rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. For config_level, the schema example format: `values[]: {condition, value}`. Rule 5 says \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\". So value inside condition entries — is `value` the structured object or raw? Ambiguous. Safest: value is the structured object: {value: ..., unit_symbol: ..., unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}. I'll do: {\"condition\": \"AC mains\", \"value\": {\"value\": {\"min\":85,\"max\":240}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}}. Hmm double \"value\" keys nested — looks odd but conforms: entry {condition, value:{value, unit_symbol,...}}. I think that matches \"structured form\" requirement. Let me use that shape.\n\nActually let me reread: rule 2: \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\" — showing {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. Rule 5: values follow same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. That suggests entry = {condition, value, unit_symbol} i.e., unit at entry level, value raw. Both readings valid. I'll choose entry-level unit: {condition, value (raw number/range/string), unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. This avoids double-nesting and matches rule 5 literally.\n\nSo config entries like:\n{\"condition\": \"AC mains\", \"value\": {\"min\": 85, \"max\": 240}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}\n\nShould footnote_markers/unit_id be omitted when null? Rule 8: omit if not present. I'll include only where meaningful; keep unit_symbol; omit unit_id (null unless certain) and footnote_markers unless present. Actually to be consistent with rule 2's strict shape, maybe include unit_id: null always? The rule says \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain\". I'll include \"unit_id\": null in structured values for clarity. Hmm — bloat. I'll include unit_id: null in type_level and model_level value objects (as rule 2 shape shows), and config entries as {condition, value, unit_symbol, unit_id}. Eh. Let me just be consistent: every value object: {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": [...]}. Config entries: {\"condition\": ..., \"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": [...]}. It's verbose but strict-compliant.\n\nHmm, that's a LOT of JSON for 30 models × 5 attributes. 150 value objects each with unit_id null... Very long. Maybe drop unit_id when null and footnote_markers when empty to reduce bloat? Rule 8: \"If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it.\" That refers to document fields, not these structural nulls. Rule 2 says shape \"MUST be an object with this shape\" — includes unit_id: null explicitly in example. I'll include unit_id: null always but omit empty footnote_markers? The example shows footnote_markers only when present. I'll include footnote_markers only when non-empty, unit_id always null-included? Mixed. Let me simplify: include \"unit_id\": null everywhere (as per strict shape), include footnote_markers only when present. OK.\n\nActually for length management with 30 models, maybe I reduce models to... no, correctness first. Let me generate.\n\nWait — reconsider whether model-level expansion for \"1 & 2\" rows into two models is right. Alternative: models per size (15 models), and class-dependent attributes (q1, q2, ratio) handled as config_level axis \"accuracy_class\"... but then per-size config combos = 15 × 2 = messy. The 30-model expansion is cleaner. Go with 30.\n\nModel ids: \"DN40 class 1\", \"DN40 class 2\", ... \"DN600 class 2\". Use format \"DN40-cl1\"? I'll use \"DN40 class 1\" — readable.\n\nmodels[] entries:\n{\"model_id\": \"DN40 class 1\", \"label\": \"Reduced bore, AC mains powered\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_size_dn\": 40, \"accuracy_class\": 1, \"bore\": \"reduced\", \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\"}}\n\nThat's a lot of repeated attributes ×30. Maybe trim attributes to {\"nominal_size_dn\": 40, \"accuracy_class\": 1} and put bore/power in the matrix table description / config. Yes — table heading covers bore/power. So attributes: nominal_size_dn, accuracy_class.\n\nmodel_level entries (attribute, unit_symbol, values[]):\n- q1: m³/h — 30 values\n- q2: m³/h — 30 values\n- q3: m³/h — 30 values\n- q4: m³/h — 30 values\n- q3_q1_ratio: null unit — 30 values\n\nEach value object: {\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": n, \"unit_id\": null}? The model_level value shape from example: {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — no unit inside (unit at attribute level). I'll follow example: {\"model\": ..., \"value\": ...} plus footnote_markers if any (none). Good — that keeps it compact.\n\nAttribute labels: \"q1\" → canonical maybe \"q1\" fine (R137 example used qmax, qt, qmin). Use q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio. Note Q2/Q1 = 1.6 and Q4/Q3 = 1.25 are fixed ratios — could add type_level notes: q2_q1_ratio: 1.6, q4_q3_ratio: 1.25 (as \"values mentioned for Q1 and Q2 are minimum values...\" — these are constraints). And \"Q3/Q1 at least 40\". I'll add type_level: q2_q1_ratio: 1.6, q4_q3_ratio: 1.25, minimum_q3_q1_ratio: 40. Good — captures the note numerically. Also note Q1, Q2 are minimum values; Q3, Q4 maximum — skip prose.\n\nmatrix_tables: two tables actually? Table 2 spans pages 3–4 with identical headers. I'll merge into ONE matrix_table entry \"General characteristics of the family of instruments — reduced bore, AC mains powered\" with 25 rows (source fidelity: \"1 & 2\" combined rows as single rows). Also the software table as second matrix_table entry.\n\nWait — should matrix_tables rows use accuracy_class \"1 & 2\" or split? For fidelity, keep as source: \"1 & 2\". The model_level already provides per-class granularity. Good.\n\nSoftware matrix table:\nname: \"Approved software versions\"\ncolumns: [\"software_part_code\", \"version\", \"checksum\"]\nrows: 7 rows as listed. All strings.\n\nNow also \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\" — checking_facilities char. type_level: checking_facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\".\n\nSoftware identification also in Table 1 — represented via matrix_table. Maybe also add